/-
The trading module's paper: algorithmic trading as the business's only income,
from research through backtest and paper to live. Checked by checks.paper-trading
(../papers.nix).
-/
open Kagamizo.Paper

def paper : Paper := {
  module := "trading",
  title := "Algorithmic trading from grounded forecasts to live markets, governed by Lean proofs",
  abstract := "The trading module is the business's income engine: algorithmic trading and published research (the owner's decision of 2026-10-31). It declares every strategy, every limit and every promotion gate in Nix, types them in Rust (the weather rules, their backtest and the Public API adapter, the last being ported from Python), and proves their safety in Lean: nothing goes live without a recorded backtest, five recorded paper days and the owner's dated decision; every order is admissible against hard caps before it reaches the exchange; every day stops at a declared loss or take-profit. The research team proposes strategies and paper trades; the owner's legal and accounting swarms review them; the owner signs every promotion and live trade.",
  authors := ["research-jingzo"],
  date := "2026-11-12",
  sections := [
    { kind := .purpose,
      body := "The business takes no work that books the owner's time: no client contracts, no consulting, nothing sold by appointment or with a live obligation. Income comes from what runs by itself: algorithmic trading and published research (the owner's decision of 2026-10-31, stated in CLAUDE.md). The trading module is the algorithmic-trading half of that. Without it the repository would have no income engine, no typed limits to keep a trading agent from over-trading, no stage gate to stop an unproven strategy from spending money, and no mechanical connection between a forecast, a contract's price and the order that risks the owner's capital. Every part of the module is declared in Nix (strategies, limits, backends, stages), proved in Lean (the stage gate, the risk rule, the limits' sanity) and, where the language rule allows, implemented in Rust (modules/trading/_rust): the weather rules, the backtest replay, the Public client and its stub, and the trade engine for every command but backtest and paper, checked against the Python on every scenario of the old check with identical output. backtest and paper still run on the Python engine, so _trade.py, _propose.py, _public.py and _weather.py remain under dated language exceptions until they move onto NautilusTrader's Rust crates.",
      cites := ["tradingagents", "fincon", "finmem"] },
    { kind := .lifecycle,
      body := "A strategy progresses through four stages declared in Nix (modules/trading/stages.nix): research, backtest, paper, live. Each promotion gates on evidence. From research to backtest: no gate except the owner's planning; the strategy starts at research and is moved to backtest by a code change. From backtest to paper: a recorded backtest with alpha above zero (P&L above the benchmark's) and a maximum drawdown of 20% or less, as `backtestCounts` computes (stages.nix line 71). From paper to live: at least five recorded paper days (paperDaysNeeded = 5) that held, the settled paper trades' P&L not below zero and their drawdown within 20% (paperHeld, from trade paper-settle's ledger), and the owner's dated decision. Paper days run on jingzo-host every day (trading.paper, modules/trading/paper.nix): trading-paper-settle at 13:43 UTC settles every recorded day whose contract Public has settled into research/paper/ledger-<strategy>.json, and trading-paper-day at 14:17 UTC records the day's proposal, priced and preflighted at Public and placed nowhere. Both run as trading-mirazo, a read-only agent of the owner's whose one grant is reading the brokerage. Lean decides that every declared strategy obeys the gate (all_well_staged, rendered by stages.nix over the declarations). The promotion evidence is declared alongside the strategy: backtest = { recorded, alpha, maxDrawdownPct }, paper = { days, from, to }, live = { decided }. The research team (research-jingzo) proposes every strategy change; review, security, the owner's legal swarm (legal-mirazo) and accounting swarm (accounting-mirazo) all pass it (agents.lifecycle.strategy). Promotions (research-jingzo proposes; legal-mirazo, accounting-mirazo and governance-mirazo review; the owner signs off; touches trade and live). Live trades (research-jingzo proposes; same reviewers; the owner signs off; touches trade, live and money). Paper trades (research-jingzo proposes; legal-mirazo and accounting-mirazo review; no owner sign-off; touches trade only). Research signals (research-jingzo proposes; governance-jingzo reviews; no owner sign-off; touches research only).",
      cites := ["tradingagents", "fincon"] },
    { kind := .pipeline,
      body := "Every push runs `nix flake check` in CI. The trading module adds checks.trading-backtest (the full integration test against a stub Public API: backtest, paper, propose, paper-day, place, and the adapter's event endpoints), checks.trading-weather (the weather signal engine's unit tests over forecast fixtures), checks.trading-stages (Lean: every strategy stage carries its evidence), checks.trading-risk (Lean: admissible guarantees; the declared limits are sane), and checks.weather-backtest (the Rust crate's own tests, run at build time). The `trade` CLI is the module's top-level package (packages.trade, a shell wrapper around the Python engine that calls governance-authorize and secretspec). NautilusTrader 1.231.0 is the packaged trading engine (packages.nautilus-trader, a built Python wheel). The weather-backtest Rust crate is packages.weather-backtest. The runner (modules/agents/runner.nix) runs on jingzo-host and schedules the research team's daily jobs: a daily weather backtest at 05:00, a paper day at 08:00, a signals review at 20:00, and a grounded-sources refresh at 05:30 — each a typed timer item (team, topic, brief, calendar), never a shell script.",
      cites := ["dolstra-thesis", "sadowski-static-analysis"] },
    { kind := .agents,
      body := "research-jingzo owns the trading module and proposes its strategy changes (kind strategy), which review, security, legal-mirazo and accounting-mirazo review and the owner signs off; upkeep writes its general code (kind code: review and security review, the owner signs off), as in the Rust port of the Python. Every kind of work has a typed lifecycle (agents/lifecycle in agents/charters.nix): research-signal (proposer research-jingzo, reviewer governance-jingzo, no owner sign-off, touches research); paper-trade (proposer research-jingzo, reviewers legal-mirazo and accounting-mirazo, no owner sign-off, touches trade); promotion (proposer research-jingzo, reviewers legal-mirazo, accounting-mirazo and governance-mirazo, owner signs off, touches trade and live); live-trade (proposer research-jingzo, reviewers legal-mirazo and accounting-mirazo, owner signs off, touches trade, live and money). The charters declare research-jingzo's goal: find and prove trading strategies that earn after costs, from research to a paper record good enough to promote; measures include a backtest and five dated paper days per strategy before promotion. The agent design the research team proposes for trading draws on the multi-agent LLM trading literature. TradingAgents (arXiv 2412.20138) structures agents as a simulated trading firm: fundamental analysts, sentiment analysts, technical analysts, and traders with varied risk profiles, plus bull and bear researcher agents debating market conditions, a risk management team monitoring exposure, and traders synthesising insights from debates and historical data. FinCon (arXiv 2407.06567) adds a manager-analyst hierarchy with conceptual verbal reinforcement through self-critiquing and selective belief propagation. FinMem (arXiv 2311.13743) introduces layered memory: profiling to customise the agent's characteristics, memory with layered message processing, and decision-making that converts memories into trades, with adjustable cognitive span. Our design adopts the debating-researcher pattern from TradingAgents (the bull and bear researchers become the research-jingzo team's analysts debating the direction) and maps onto our charters as follows: the fundamental and technical analysts map to research-jingzo's research-synthesist persona; the risk management role maps to the governance-mirazo team's chief-financial-officer; the trader role that places orders is never an LLM agent — only the deterministic `trade place` command, authorised by governance-authorize and gated by the strategy's declared stage, may send a spend action. This follows the owner's rule that no agent may spend (CLAUDE.md: 'what the owner does by hand is a closed, typed set'). The FinMem layered-memory pattern is not adopted: the deterministic weather rules (the `weather rules` of the _weather.py engine, ported to Rust in modules/trading/_rust/weather) replace any model that would 'talk' — no LLM ever prices a contract. The FinCon manager-analyser hierarchy with self-critiquing is partially adopted: the research-synthesist acts as the manager, the finance-fpa-analyst as the analyst (modelling returns and drawdowns), and the review by governance-jingzo acts as the self-critiquing mechanism — but again, no LLM evaluates a trade's pricing. The newer work fixes where the boundary of the agents sits. Steward (Feng et al., arXiv 2610.30557) lets nine role-specialised agents decide jointly while a protected Risk Manager audits every target position and the safeguards live in an invariant core drawn in the type system, which the agents may not change; our counterpart is the Lean-proved limits and stage gates, which no team can merge (kind live-trade and money-flow need the owner). SwingGuard (Su et al., arXiv 2610.12988) conditions strategy generation on the current risk and runs risk governance on its own faster timescale; ours is `admissible`, checked at proposal and again at placement. So the adopted roles are: analysts (the research loop and the deterministic weather rules, which replace a model wherever a rule can price the question), bull and bear debate inside research-jingzo, a trader that only proposes, risk as the typed limits rather than an agent, and legal and accounting review of every trade.",
      cites := ["tradingagents", "fincon", "finmem", "steward", "swingguard"] },
    { kind := .tools,
      body := "Public's API (kagamizo-public, modules/trading/_rust/public, replacing _public.py): reads bars, preflights orders, lists option chains and event contracts, over TLS, typed request and response by response; public-stub serves the same routes for the tests. Every request is bounded (kagamizo_public::endpoints, checks.public-api-bound): the client's table is Public's whole API, all 30 endpoints of the pinned CLI's generated client, each the governance action of its CLI command with that action's effect; the reader client, which every command but place uses, can call no endpoint that writes or spends, and only `place` builds a placer, which adds the single-leg order alone. An order is read back until Public's own lifecycle says it ended (OrderStatus: filled, cancelled, expired, rejected or replaced; a partial fill is still working). trade paper-settle settles recorded paper days by each contract's settled outcome (SettledOutcome YES, NO or OTHER), and trading-host starts the host's units with no shell. Only `trade place` sends an order that spends money, and it does so only after governance-authorize and secretspec have passed. The adapter calls token, account, instrument, bars, preflight, option_expirations, option_chain, greeks, event_contract_summary, event_contracts, event_contract_details, event_contract_bars, quotes, place and order — every method maps to one Public HTTP endpoint. Only `place` places. NautilusTrader: its Rust crates (0.67.0) run a backtest with no Python, as checks.nautilus-probe shows (a simulated venue, SPY quotes, the EMA-cross example, every order filled; the API mapped in _json/nautilus-rust-api.json), so the Rust trade binary will drive them directly; until then the Python wheel (packages.nautilus-trader, 1.231.0) is the core trading engine for backtest simulation and paper trading on a simulated venue, with a simulated balance. It runs on any strategy kind (ema-cross, agent-option, event-rule) through the `trade` CLI. The weather rules (kagamizo_weather Rust crate, ported from modules/trading/_weather.py): deterministic, tier-0 rule engine that parses a question into a typed shape, computes a probability from forecasts and climatology, and computes the edge against a contract's price. No model that talks. The weather backtest (kagamizo_weather_backtest Rust crate): replays the weather rules over historical days against the declared limits, computing P&L, alpha, max drawdown and hit rate. Fetchers for Open-Meteo (free, no account needed) and the National Weather Service's api.weather.gov (free, needs a User-Agent header) are built into _weather.py, ported to Rust.",
      cites := ["tradingagents"] },
    { kind := .data,
      body := "Forecasts (_json/forecasts.json): a fixture with two cities (New York and Chicago), daily observed and forecast records for November 2026, and per-month climatology means and standard deviations for precipitation and snow. Used by the weather rules' unit tests and the integration checks. Backtest days (_json/backtest-days.json): a fixture of three days (2026-11-05 to 2026-11-07) with markets (question, outcome, YES and NO prices, close time), forecasts and observed values. Used by the weather-backtest crate's tests. NWS climate reports: the National Weather Service publishes daily climate data (observed high, low, precipitation) through its API and as text products; the Kalshi weather contracts settle on these. Fetched live through _weather.py's NWS parser; no fixture yet. Kalshi prices through Public: the Public adapter lists event contracts from Kalshi's exchange through Public's event-contracts endpoints; each contract carries a YES and a NO ask price in dollars from 0.00 to 1.00, the implied probability the market places on that side. Fetched live through the adapter; the stub (_stub_public.py) provides three fake events for the integration checks.",
      cites := ["dolstra-thesis"] },
    { kind := .design,
      body := "The module follows the repository's three-layer architecture: Lean states the rules and proves what they guarantee, Rust implements against them, Nix pins and packages both and runs the proofs as checks. Limits (_lean/Risk.lean, limits.nix): the trading limits are declared per backend, each a typed submodule (seedUsd, maxPremiumUsd, maxContractPriceUsd, maxOpenPositions, dailyLossStopUsd, dailyTakeProfitUsd, allowed kinds). The Lean function `admissible(l, d, o)` conjoins five guards: the kind is allowed, the loss cap fits, the book has room, the day is below its loss stop and below its take-profit. Five theorems in Risk.lean derive what admissible guarantees. The limits are rendered into a Lean file and decided sane by `declared_sane`. Stages (_lean/Stages.lean, stages.nix): the four-stage gate is typed (Stage: research, backtest, paper, live) with a rank order. A Strategy structure carries name, stage, backtested, paperDays and decided. The Lean function `wellStaged` checks that no strategy is paper without being backtested, and no strategy live without enough paper days and the owner's decision. Two theorems in Stages.lean derive what wellStaged guarantees. The weather rules are implemented first in Python (_weather.py) and ported to Rust (kagamizo_weather in _rust/weather/) with the same test fixtures; the backtest (kagamizo_weather_backtest in _rust/weather-backtest/) replays the rules over historical days. The Public client and its stub are Rust (kagamizo-public, public-stub), and trade-run (modules/trading/_rust/trade) is the engine the wrapper calls for strategies, account, bars, propose, paper-day and place. The `trade` CLI bridges Nix, Python, Rust and Public, and the governance-authorize call ensures no agent places a spend action without the owner.",
      cites := ["tradingagents", "gonthier-reflection", "lean4"] },
    { kind := .guarantees,
      body := "Every Lean theorem in the trading module, by name, and what it guarantees. Kagamizo.Readiness (Readiness.lean): `live_needs_every_step`: in a sound readiness, a strategy at stage live implies every step of the live lifecycle (sign-in, account, market data, signals, risk, preflight, place, read-back, cancel, position, settlement, reconcile, ledger, approval, the agents, observability; the agents run daily on the host's small quantized model at no cost, research --local, which proves their whole lifecycle but not their judgement) has run against Public's real backend, every paper day or once by a canary order, so going live is a setting and never the first time a piece of wiring meets the real backend; today place, read-back, cancel and position are proved only against the stub, and reconcile, ledger and approval are not built, so no strategy can be live. Kagamizo.Stages (Stages.lean): `live_has_evidence`: a live strategy was backtested, papered long enough (paperDays >= 5), held on paper (paperHeld) and decided by the owner. `paper_loss_blocks_live`: a strategy whose paper trading did not hold is never well staged as live, however many paper days it ran. `paper_was_backtested`: a paper strategy was backtested. `all_well_staged` (rendered, decide): every declared strategy obeys the stage gate. Kagamizo.Risk (Risk.lean): `admissible_parts`: breaks an admissible order into its five conjuncts (kind allowed, loss capped, book open, day under stop, day under take-profit). `admissible_loss_capped`: an admissible order can lose at most the premium cap. `admissible_allowed`: an admissible order is of an allowed kind. `day_loss_bounded`: even after an admissible order, the day's loss stays under the stop plus one premium cap. `stopped_day_refuses`: once the stop is reached, nothing is admissible. `taken_profit_day_refuses`: once the take-profit is reached, nothing is admissible. `admissible_under_take_profit`: an admissible order was proposed on a day still under its take-profit. `full_book_refuses`: with one position open and the limit one, nothing more is admissible. `declared_sane` (rendered, decide): the declared limits never violate the sanity invariants (maxPremiumUsd <= seedUsd, dailyLossStopUsd <= seedUsd, dailyTakeProfitUsd <= seedUsd, maxContractPriceUsd <= maxPremiumUsd, dailyLossStopUsd + maxPremiumUsd <= 2 * seedUsd).",
      cites := ["lean4", "gonthier-reflection"] },
    { kind := .performance,
      body := "Three perf targets are measured by rebuilding from scratch (modules/perf/targets.nix). trading-risk (check: trading-risk, module: trading, kind: build): Lean proving what admissible guarantees (omega over Nat) and deciding the declared limits sane; moves with the number of declared limits backends. trading-stages (check: trading-stages, module: trading, kind: build): Lean deciding every strategy's stage carries its evidence; moves with the number of declared strategies. trading-weather (check: trading-weather, module: trading, kind: build): The weather signal engine's unit tests over forecast fixtures (parsers, probabilities, edge); moves with the fixture size and the number of test cases. nautilus-probe (package: nautilus-probe, module: trading, kind: build): cargo build --release of NautilusTrader's Rust engine, 0.67 and about 180 crates, with rustc 1.99.1 from rust-overlay since nixpkgs ships 1.95; it is the heaviest build in the module, so the weather crates' check excludes it, and it moves with Nautilus's version and enabled features. Its check runs the probe to fills: 745 quotes, 12 orders, all filled. The backtest integration check (trading-backtest) is measured only as a CI check, not a perf target.",
      cites := ["lean4"] },
    { kind := .priorWork,
      body := "TradingAgents (Xiao et al., arXiv 2412.20138) proposes a multi-agent LLM stock trading framework inspired by trading firms, with specialised agents (fundamental analysts, sentiment analysts, technical analysts, traders with varied risk profiles) and debating bull and bear researchers. FinCon (Yu et al., arXiv 2407.06567) adds a manager-analyst communication hierarchy with conceptual verbal reinforcement through self-critiquing and selective belief propagation. FinMem (Yu et al., arXiv 2311.13743) introduces layered memory (profiling, memory with layered processing, decision-making) with adjustable cognitive span for financial decision-making. Our work departs from all three by replacing the pricing LLM with deterministic, rule-based pricing (the weather rules: a normal CDF computed from forecasts, not from a model that talks) and by making the gate to spend a Lean-proved Nix declaration, not an agent's internal decision. The Kelly criterion (Kelly, 1956, Bell System Technical Journal) gave the first formal treatment of optimal bet sizing under known odds; our limits adopt a conservative variant (a hard premium cap, one open position, a daily stop and take-profit) that bounds the worst case rather than optimising for growth. The Brier score (Brier, 1950, Monthly Weather Review) measures forecast calibration; our weather rules forecast probabilities through a normal CDF whose parameters (sigma_f with lead time, forecast_precip_sd) are declared and tested, so the edge is a mechanical property of the forecast, not of the market. Nix (Dolstra, 2006) and NixOS (Dolstra, Löh and Pierron, 2010) provide the hermetic, purely functional builds that let a Lean check be a derivation whose existence is the proof. Lean 4 (de Moura and Ullrich, 2021) and proof by reflection (Gonthier, 2008) let decidable properties of rendered facts be settled by the kernel. On evaluation, Hindcast (Ye et al., arXiv 2607.14051) replays resolved prediction markets to score LLM forecasters without look-ahead, which is the method of our weather backtest: forecasts as of before close, settled on the recorded outcome. Li et al. (arXiv 2609.32885) score LLM forecasts on prediction markets by the Brier score, the measure of calibration our weather rules are to be judged by alongside P&L.",
      cites := ["tradingagents", "fincon", "finmem", "steward", "swingguard", "hindcast", "brier-llm-markets", "kelly", "brier", "dolstra-thesis", "nixos-jfp", "lean4", "gonthier-reflection"] },
    { kind := .gaps,
      body := "backtest and paper over bars still run on the Python engine (_trade.py and NautilusTrader's wheel), which imports _propose.py, _public.py and _weather.py, so those stay under their dated exceptions until the two commands run on NautilusTrader's Rust crates. No live data source is wired through the Nix module: the weather rules fetch live from Open-Meteo and the NWS through the Python _weather.py, but the research.dataSources catalog (research-jingzo/grounded-sources) is not yet typed, so the strategy's declared source is never checked against a declaration. There is no recorded backtest for the weather-rules strategy: the module's backtest-days.json is a fixture, not a live backtest run. The weather-backtest crate is built and tested but not yet wired into the stages gate: its result is not read by stages.nix. The Public account is connected but holds no money: by the owner's decision nothing is deposited until paper trading is validated, and funding it by debit card is not yet verified. The agent-option strategy reads its decisions from the research directory, which only the research team's daily jobs will write, and those jobs run only once the host's agent runner is turned on (agents.runner.enable, off today). No paper day has been recorded for any strategy. Lean does not yet decide the backtest counters: `wellStaged` reads a Bool (backtested) and a Nat (paperDays) that the Nix renders from the declaration, not from any external source of truth.",
      cites := ["tradingagents"] }
  ],
  citations := [
    { key := "tradingagents", title := "TradingAgents: Multi-Agents LLM Financial Trading Framework", authors := ["Yijia Xiao", "Edward Sun", "Di Luo", "Wei Wang"], year := 2024, source := .arxiv "2412.20138" },
